    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-20528" textLabel="tricin synthase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9XGP7" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="697.7802124023438" centerY="683.9025268554688" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">The |FRAME: G-58318 ROMT-15| gene was one of 19 genes an &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase (OMT) domain found in the rice genome. It was one of only two class I OMTs, which resemble |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.104 EC 2.1.1.104, caffeoyl-CoA &lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase|. Real-time PCR showed that the gene was expressed in stem and roots more than in other tissues |CITS: [16412485]|. The gene was later cloned with as a glutathione &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-transferase (GST) tag and expressed in |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, and the recombinant protein was purified. Assays with potential substrates showed that the enzyme was active with both |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA| and flavonoids. had the highest activity with |FRAME: CPD-21292 7,8-dihydroxyflavone|, followed by |FRAME: MYRICETIN myricetin|, |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-caffeoyl-CoA|, |FRAME: 5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE luteolin|, |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin|, and |FRAME: 5-HYDROXY-FERULIC-ACID 5-hydroxyferulate| |CITS: [17943312]|. The enzyme could not utilize the flavanones |FRAME: NARINGENIN-CMPD (2&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-naringenin|, |FRAME: CPD-474 (+)-taxifolin| and |FRAME: CPD-6994 (2S)-eriodictyol|, the flavone |FRAME: CPD-431 apigenin|, and the flavonol |FRAME: CPD1F-90 kaempferol|, suggesting that 2,3-double bond and hydroxyl groups at positions 3&apos; and 4&apos; are both required for catalytic activity |CITS: [17943312]|. When acting on most flavonoids, the enzyme catalyzed a 3&apos;-hydroxylation. However, after acting on |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin| and on |FRAME: MYRICETIN myricetin|, forming |FRAME: CPD-12571 selgin| and |FRAME: CPD-8605 laricitrin|, respectively, the enzyme catalyzed the 5&apos;-hydroxylation of the prduct, forming |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| and |FRAME: SYRINGETIN syringetin|, respectively. This activity is known as |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.175 EC 2.1.1.175, tricin synthase|. Unlike the wheat enzyme |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.169 EC 2.1.1.169, tricetin 3&amp;prime;,4&amp;prime;,5&amp;prime;-&lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-trimethyltransferase|, the enzyme did not catalyze a third hydroxylation reaction on |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| |CITS: [17943312]|. The activity of the enzyme is metal-dependent, and Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; is the best cation for catalytic activity, followed by Co&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; and Mn&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; |CITS: [17943312]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-20528" />
